<.shell title="Buttons and tags" current={:components}>
  <h1>Buttons and tags</h1>
  <p class="lead">Flat buttons with a hard bottom edge, and small tags for status.</p>

  <h2>Button kinds</h2>
  <div class="demo">
    <.form_actions>
      <.button variant={:primary}>Save settings</.button>
      <.button>Cancel</.button>
      <.button variant={:success}>Create letter</.button>
      <.button variant={:danger}>Delete clause</.button>
      <.button variant={:link}>More information</.button>
    </.form_actions>
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Primary</strong>: the one main action on a page.</li>
    <li><strong>Secondary</strong>: everything else, including Cancel and Reset.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Success</strong>: an action that creates or adds, where green helps scanning a table.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Danger</strong>: deletes or removes. Ask first when it cannot be undone.</li>
    <li><strong>Link</strong>: a quiet action inside text or beside other buttons.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="spec">
    In the app: <code>&lt;.button variant=&#123;:primary&#125;&gt;</code>. With no variant, a button is secondary. Give it
    <code>navigate</code>
    or <code>href</code>
    for a link that looks like a button.
  </p>

  <h2>Sizes</h2>
  <div class="demo">
    <.form_actions>
      <.button variant={:primary}>Default, md text</.button>
      <.button variant={:primary} size={:sm}>Small, sm text</.button>
      <.button size={:sm}>Small secondary</.button>
      <.button variant={:success} size={:sm}>Create letter</.button>
      <.button variant={:danger} size={:sm}>Remove</.button>
    </.form_actions>
  </div>
  <p class="spec">
    Default is 36px tall for forms and page actions. Small is 24px, for table rows and toolbars. Large, <code>size=&#123;:lg&#125;</code>, is only for the one action on the door's scan page and the ID card check.
  </p>

  <h2>States</h2>
  <p>
    Every kind in every state, frozen so you can compare them on any screen. Try the real ones
    above: on a phone, press and hold to see the pressed state.
  </p>
  <.table id="button-states" rows={button_kinds()}>
    <:col :let={{_variant, label}} label="Kind">{label}</:col>
    <:col :let={{variant, _label}} label="Default">
      <.button variant={variant}>Save</.button>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{variant, _label}} label="Hover">
      <.button variant={variant} class="is-hover" tabindex="-1">Save</.button>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{variant, _label}} label="Pressed">
      <.button variant={variant} class="is-active" tabindex="-1">Save</.button>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{variant, _label}} label="Focus">
      <.button variant={variant} class="is-focus" tabindex="-1">Save</.button>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{variant, _label}} label="Disabled">
      <.button variant={variant} disabled>Save</.button>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{variant, _label}} label="Busy">
      <.button variant={variant} disabled><.spinner>Saving…</.spinner></.button>
    </:col>
  </.table>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hover</strong> darkens the button, only on devices with a mouse or trackpad.</li>
    <li><strong>Pressed</strong> drops the button 2px onto its edge, on every device.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Focus</strong>
      is yellow with a dark edge for every kind, so keyboard users see one signal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Disabled</strong> is a last resort. Prefer an error that says what is missing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Busy</strong> shows a spinner and says what it is doing, for work that takes more
      than a second.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Button rows</h2>
  <div class="demo">
    <.form_actions>
      <.button variant={:primary}>Save settings</.button>
      <.button>Cancel</.button>
      <:trailing>
        <.button variant={:danger}>Delete team</.button>
      </:trailing>
    </.form_actions>
  </div>
  <p class="spec">
    The main action first. A destructive action sits apart, on the right. In the app: <code>&lt;.form_actions&gt;</code>, with the destructive action in its
    <code>&lt;:trailing&gt;</code>
    slot.
  </p>

  <h2>Tags</h2>
  <p>
    Five status tags, the three D4H activity kinds as a tag or a marker, and an outlined tag for D4H's own tags. Tinted tags say what something is. Solid tags ask for action.
  </p>
  <.table id="tag-kinds" rows={tag_kinds()}>
    <:col :let={t} label="Tag" class="whitespace-nowrap">
      <span :if={t.kind == :kinds} class="badges">
        <.badge kind={:incident}>Incident</.badge>
        <.badge kind={:exercise}>Exercise</.badge>
        <.badge kind={:event}>Event</.badge>
      </span>
      <span :if={t.kind == :markers} class="badges">
        <.activity_kind activity={
          %{activity_kind: "incident", is_published: true, tracking_number: nil}
        } />
        <.activity_kind activity={
          %{activity_kind: "exercise", is_published: true, tracking_number: nil}
        } />
        <.activity_kind activity={
          %{activity_kind: "event", is_published: true, tracking_number: nil}
        } />
      </span>
      <.badge :if={t.kind not in [:kinds, :markers]} kind={t.kind}>{t.sample}</.badge>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={t} label="Look" class="w-px whitespace-nowrap">{t.look}</:col>
    <:col :let={t} label="Means">{t.means}</:col>
    <:col :let={t} label="Examples">{t.examples}</:col>
  </.table>
  <ul>
    <li>One to three words. The words carry the meaning; the colour helps people scan.</li>
    <li>At most one status tag per row or item. If two apply, show the more urgent one.</li>
    <li>
      Amber and red are for things a person should act on. If everything is amber, nothing is.
    </li>
    <li>
      A column where every row has a tag is too loud. Show the D4H kind there as a marker, and
      fold D4H's primary and secondary hours tags into the Hours column as a word.
    </li>
    <li>
      A normal state gets no tag. Show "Draft" on an unpublished activity, never "Published" on the rest.
    </li>
    <li>Codes such as tracking numbers are mono text, not tags.</li>
    <li>
      Several tags together sit in a <code>.badges</code>
      group, so they wrap with a gap and never touch.
    </li>
    <li>Tags are labels, not buttons. Nothing happens when you select one.</li>
    <li>
      In the app: <code>&lt;.badge kind=&#123;:warning&#125;&gt;</code>, and
      <code>&lt;.activity_kind activity=&#123;…&#125; /&gt;</code>
      for the marker.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <div class="demo">
    <p class="paragraph">
      In a table row: Casey Dhillon, Wilderness First Aid
      <.badge kind={:warning}>12 days</.badge>
    </p>
    <p class="paragraph">
      Beside a heading: Missing hiker, Stawamus Chief
      <.badge kind={:incident}>Incident</.badge>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Focus</h2>
  <div class="demo">
    <p class="paragraph">
      Press Tab. Everything you can reach gets the yellow focus, the same in both modes: <.a href="#">a link</.a>, <.button size={
        :sm
      }>a button</.button>, and
      <input type="text" class="inline-block max-w-40" aria-label="An input" />.
    </p>
  </div>
</.shell>
